From 9a9e59b9c8d61ce14b38d30dfa72e9dba141f583 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luissalet <luismasc16@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 02:50:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 26/36] Add the ComfyUI binding layer and its transport

The binding layer, not a node editor: a template is a graph plus a manifest,
and a recipe is written into it through declared bindings that resolve by
node title first and node id second, which is the documented fix for the
brittleness a re-exported workflow otherwise causes. Occurrences a recipe
does not use are deleted and the chain closed over them, so node ids never
move and ComfyUI's execution cache survives between submissions.

Everything a recipe could be refused for lives in one place: a template
whose node pack is missing is reported by pack name, a model this install
does not have is reported with the nearest one it does, and a recipe
carrying something the chosen template cannot express is refused instead of
rendered without it. None of them reach /prompt.

The transport speaks ComfyUI's legacy HTTP and WebSocket API, which every
install has with nothing extra installed. It takes its fetch and its socket
as arguments so it stays free of Electron and can be tested against a fake
server; a socket that never opens or that drops falls back to polling
/history, which is the authority on completion either way.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
